CAT = "sgsecLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
# Private Security Industry Act 2007 (PSIA, 2020 RevEd as amended to Act 23 of 2025), the Private
# Security Industry (Conduct) Regulations 2009 (as amended by S 590/2025), the PLRD Licensing
# Conditions for Security Officers (revised 12 December 2024) and the employment rights summary in
# the PLRD refresher e-quiz training package. No fines or fees are asked.
Q = [
("Under the PSIA, which paid task makes a person a security officer who needs a licence?",
 "Regulating road traffic", ["Repairing the building's CCTV", "Cleaning the car park at night", "Sorting tenants' parcels and mail"],
 "Section 13 lists patrolling or guarding property, surveillance to prevent theft, acting as a bodyguard or bouncer, screening people seeking entry, and regulating traffic under the Road Traffic Act. Repairing CCTV is technical work."),
("A man advertises online that he will guard homes for a fee, but holds no licence yet. Under the PSIA this is:",
 "Already an offence", ["Allowed until his first paid job", "Allowed if he charges nothing", "Only a matter for his clients"],
 "Section 14(1)(b) bars anyone from advertising or holding out that they will carry out security officer functions for reward without a licence, not just from doing the work."),
("Which of these is part of the PSIA's definition of a bouncer's work?",
 "Removing people for their conduct", ["Selling tickets at the entrance", "Driving guests safely home", "Checking food hygiene at the bar"],
 "Section 2 defines a bouncer as someone who, as part of regular duties at a place of entertainment, screens people seeking entry, controls or monitors behaviour, or removes people for behavioural reasons."),
("Under the PSIA, a frisk search means:",
 "Hands run over outer clothing", ["Asking the person to undress", "Searching inside worn clothing", "Scanning a bag with no one there"],
 "Section 2 describes a frisk search as quickly running the hands over a person's outer clothing, plus examining items the person removes voluntarily."),
("For how long can a licence under the PSIA be valid at most?",
 "Five years", ["One year", "Three years", "Ten years"],
 "Section 23 says a licence is valid for the period stated in it, which may not exceed five years."),
("A security officer is charged with a prescribed offence and served a notice of immediate suspension. What must they do?",
 "Stop all guard work at once", ["Finish this month's shifts first", "Work only at their usual site", "Carry on until the trial ends"],
 "Section 24(5) says the licensee must immediately cease carrying out any function of a security officer once the notice of immediate suspension is served."),
("After an immediate suspension notice, how long does the officer have to ask the licensing officer to review the decision?",
 "14 days", ["7 days", "30 days", "3 months"],
 "Section 24(6) allows an application for review within 14 days after the notice of immediate suspension is served."),
("What is the longest period for which the licensing officer may suspend a licence under section 24(2)?",
 "Six months", ["Three months", "One year", "Two years"],
 "Where there is no cause serious enough to revoke a licence, section 24(2) lets the licensing officer suspend it for up to six months, censure the licensee, or impose other directions."),
("An application for a security officer's licence is refused. To whom can the applicant appeal?",
 "The Minister", ["The State Courts", "The Commissioner of Police", "The Ministry of Manpower"],
 "Section 26(1) allows an appeal to the Minister within 14 days after being told of the refusal, and the Minister's decision is final."),
("Which of these may the licensing officer treat as a sign that an applicant is not fit and proper?",
 "Past use of harassing tactics", ["Living in rented housing", "Changing jobs often", "Being under 30 years old"],
 "Section 21(8) lists associating with criminals, dishonesty or lack of integrity, harassing tactics, mental disorder, and being an undischarged bankrupt among the matters the licensing officer may consider."),
("An applicant hides a material fact on the licence application form. Under section 21(7) this is:",
 "An offence", ["Allowed if corrected later", "Only a reason for delay", "A matter for the employer"],
 "Section 21(7) makes it an offence to give false particulars or to suppress a material fact intentionally when applying for a licence."),
("Under the licensing conditions, a security officer may be employed as a security officer by at most:",
 "Two employers at once", ["One employer at once", "Three employers at once", "Any number of employers"],
 "Condition 3 of the Licensing Conditions for Security Officers says the licensee shall not be employed as a security officer by more than two employers at any one time."),
("A licensed officer aged 64 works for a condominium that is not a security agency. What does the licensing condition require?",
 "A yearly medical fitness certificate", ["A new licence every single year", "A supervisor present on every shift", "Day shifts only after turning 60"],
 "Condition 1 requires a licensee above 60 who works for an employer that is not a licensed security agency to be certified medically fit before deployment and every year after."),
("Under the Progressive Wage Model, which jobs may a Senior Security Officer perform?",
 "Their own and lower grades' jobs", ["Any job at any grade at all", "Only jobs of the grades above", "Only the Security Officer's jobs"],
 "The licensing conditions say officers of a higher grade can perform the job functions of lower grades but not the other way round, and no one may perform functions above their grade."),
("Before patrolling with a patrol dog, a security officer must have:",
 "The Licensing Officer's written approval", ["The client's spoken agreement", "A dog licence from the town council", "A note from the site supervisor"],
 "Condition 6 says a licensee shall not patrol with a patrol dog without the prior written approval of the Licensing Officer. A patrol dog includes dogs that detect explosives or drugs."),
("Which officer needs the WSQ certificate for screening using an X-ray machine before doing the job?",
 "One running a bag X-ray machine", ["One checking staff passes by eye", "One keeping the occurrence book", "One manning a car park barrier"],
 "Condition 4 requires the WSQ certification \"Conduct security screening using X-Ray machine\" before operating X-ray machines, unless the Licensing Officer exempts the officer."),
("Under the PSIA, which act against a security officer on duty carries the heaviest maximum penalty?",
 "Voluntarily causing hurt", ["Insulting words meant to distress", "Using criminal force on them", "Sending abusive text messages"],
 "Sections 17A to 17C protect officers on duty. Voluntarily causing hurt (s 17B) carries up to five years' jail, more than criminal force (s 17A) or harassment (s 17C)."),
("For an offence of harassing a security officer under section 17C, the offender must:",
 "Know the target was acting as one", ["Have harassed the officer twice", "Work at the same premises", "Also have used physical force"],
 "Section 17E says no offence is committed unless the person knew or ought reasonably to have known the target was a security officer acting in that capacity."),
("Under the Conduct Regulations, which is an offence for an officer on duty?",
 "Sleeping while deployed", ["Drinking water at the post", "Asking a visitor for ID", "Writing in the occurrence book"],
 "Regulation 7A(1) says an officer carrying out security functions must not sleep, drink or be under the influence of alcohol, leave the post without valid reason, or use threatening or abusive language."),
("An officer tells a client he holds certifications he never earned. Under the Conduct Regulations this is:",
 "A false representation", ["Allowed if he is experienced", "Only his employer's concern", "An issue only at renewal"],
 "Regulation 7A(2) bars a licensed security officer from giving any false representation about their level of training, skill or qualification."),
("A tenant at the officer's site reports that her bag was stolen in the lobby. What does regulation 7A require?",
 "A prompt response to her", ["Telling her to phone 999 herself", "Waiting for the next patrol round", "Helping only if she lives there"],
 "Regulation 7A(1)(e) requires an officer to respond promptly to anyone within the deployed premises who asks for help after injury, damage to property or loss of property there."),
("A public officer from a statutory board is carrying out an inspection at the site. The security officer must not:",
 "Deliberately hinder the inspection", ["Ask to see the officer's pass", "Tell the building management", "Record the visit in the log"],
 "Regulation 3 forbids a licensed security officer from intentionally interfering with, hindering or obstructing a public officer or statutory board employee carrying out a duty under written law."),
("Under MOM's rules summarised by PLRD, an officer's salary must be paid within how many days after the salary period ends?",
 "7 days", ["3 days", "14 days", "30 days"],
 "The refresher training package states that salary must be paid at least once a month and within 7 days after the end of the salary period."),
("To claim paid sick leave, an officer must have worked for the same employer for at least:",
 "3 months", ["1 month", "6 months", "12 months"],
 "Paid sick and hospitalisation leave start after 3 months' continuous service with the same employer, and the employee must inform or try to inform the employer within 48 hours of the absence."),
]
